WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:07.149
United’s flight 3411 was scheduled to leave
Chicago O’hare airport at 5:40 pm on Sunday,

00:00:07.149 --> 00:00:11.270
April 9th to Louisville, Kentucky, but after
dragging a passenger off the flight and a

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:16.510
two hour delay, it turned into one of the
biggest PR disasters an airline has ever seen.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:21.940
Of course, the impetus of this entire news
story is that airlines sell more seats than

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:25.130
they have—its just better business.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:29.190
With any flight, a certain number of people
won’t show up—that’s called the no-show

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:30.190
rate.

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:34.620
Flying with an empty seat is a lost opportunity
for an airline to make money—even if someone

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.430
did buy a ticket for that seat—so many airlines
have instilled complicated systems to predict

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:41.820
how many people will miss their flight.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.140
That no-show rate varies hugely by route and
date.

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:50.880
In United’s network, Chicago, where flight
3411 left from, likely has a high no-show

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.160
rate since it is their hub.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.140
A large percentage of the passengers taking
a flight from Chicago connect from another

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:02.090
flight and, since 1/4 of US domestic flights
arrive late, many people miss connections.

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:07.450
In addition, there are a ton of competing
airlines and modes of transportation from

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:11.959
Chicago so it’s not a huge deal for passengers
to change their plans to leave a few hours

00:01:11.959 --> 00:01:13.070
or days later.

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:19.499
On a flight from, say, Bangor, Maine, a small
rural airport, to Washington DC, passengers

00:01:19.499 --> 00:01:23.490
are much less likely to miss or decide not
to show up for their flight.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:27.719
No passengers are connecting through Bangor
onto this flight to DC and there are also

00:01:27.719 --> 00:01:29.649
very few alternatives.

00:01:29.649 --> 00:01:34.579
With only 10-15 flights a day and no train
service, missing a flight out of Bangor is

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:37.999
much more inconvenient than one out of Chicago.

00:01:37.999 --> 00:01:43.419
Flights on Sunday nights, like flight 3411,
are also likely to have a lower no-show rate

00:01:43.419 --> 00:01:46.490
since travellers are wary to get back to work
on Monday.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:50.709
There’s no good public data on how many
passengers miss their flights—it’s a closely

00:01:50.709 --> 00:01:55.429
guarded secret—but this data is used by
airlines to build models that predict how

00:01:55.429 --> 00:01:58.469
many seats they can safely oversell.

00:01:58.469 --> 00:02:01.130
The good news is that these models are getting
better.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:07.810
In 1990 US airlines denied boarding to about
16 out of every 10,000 passengers while today

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:11.170
its down to 9 out of every 10,000 passengers.

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:15.710
With better and better data and smarter and
smarter programs, these models are constantly

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:20.530
improving to let airlines sell these nonexistent
seats more safely.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:25.090
In the end, what airlines pay to compensate
those half a million yearly passengers who

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:30.130
are bumped from their flights is far less
than the hundreds of millions of dollars they

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:35.920
can make overbooking—until of course, it
turns into a worldwide PR disaster.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.420
So how can you be sure that you won’t be
bumped?

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:42.810
When airlines do have to involuntarily select
individuals to offload, they’ll often use

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:47.150
a hierarchical system—choosing the individuals
that supposedly have the least merit to be

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:50.730
on the flight or least value to the airline
on paper.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:56.100
Normally a computer will pick those who checked
in last and have no or low frequent flier

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:57.100
status.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:02.310
So, if you check in early, even online, select
a seat, and get your boarding pass, you’re

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:04.830
much more likely to fly on your flight.

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:09.410
In addition, having a frequent flier account,
even if you don’t use it much, will improve

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:12.900
your odds over the majority of people who
don’t have one at all.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:18.460
You can also fly airlines that overbook less:
JetBlue had to involuntarily deny boarding

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:23.630
to only 18 out of its 21 million passengers
in 2013.

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:27.990
Since they sell mostly sell inflexible tickets
with fewer connections, passengers are much

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:33.420
more likely to show up for their flight so
JetBlue has a blanket policy not to overbook

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:34.420
flights.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:37.940
What you have to remember with airlines, however,
is that they’re constantly trying to find

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:42.170
ways to make more money per seat so that they
can reduce ticket prices.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:48.220
That’s not to say they’re altruistic organizations,
they just recognize that the number one determinant

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:52.740
to whether someone will fly their airline
is that one number—the ticket price—so

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:58.920
they’ll take almost any opportunity to reduce
it in order to gain a competitive advantage.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:03.100
Time and time again it’s been proven that
the majority of consumers want cheaper flights

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:09.060
more than they want a better travel experience—that’s
why budget airlines are making such a disruption—so

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:15.220
this whole trend of unbundled airfares, fees
for everything, and even overbooking is all

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:21.400
in response to what the consumer as one entity
wants—cheap flights.

